  <section id="chto-eto">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>Normally blood passes from an artery to a vein through a network of the tiniest vessels — capillaries. They dampen the pressure and deliver oxygen to tissue. In an <strong>arteriovenous malformation</strong> there is no capillary network: arteries connect straight to veins and form a tangle — the <strong>nidus</strong>.</p>
    <p>Imagine mains water pressure fed straight into a thin garden hose, bypassing the reducer. Veins are not built for arterial pressure: they widen, their walls thin and may rupture. And the surrounding brain gets less blood, because it takes the short route into the tangle.</p>
    <p>An AVM is a congenital feature of vessel development. It is not related to lifestyle and cannot be prevented. It shows itself more often in young people but can appear at any age.</p>
    <Scheme />
  </section>

  <section id="simptomy">
    <h2>Symptoms</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Brain haemorrhage</strong> — the most dangerous presentation. A sudden severe headache, vomiting, weakness of an arm or leg, speech problems, loss of consciousness.</li>
      <li><strong>Epileptic seizures</strong> — the second most common presentation. Sometimes seizures are the first and only sign.</li>
      <li><strong>Headaches</strong>, often on one side.</li>
      <li><strong>Gradually increasing weakness</strong>, numbness, problems with vision or speech — when the brain around the AVM lacks blood.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>An AVM is often found by chance — on an MRI done for another reason.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="diagnostika">
    <h2>Diagnosis</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Brain MRI</strong> — shows the malformation, its location relative to important brain areas and traces of previous bleeds.</li>
      <li><strong>CT</strong> — the first test if a bleed is suspected.</li>
      <li><strong>CT or MR angiography</strong> — shows the vessels feeding and draining the AVM.</li>
      <li><strong>Cerebral angiography</strong> — the gold standard. Contrast is injected through an arterial puncture and the flow is seen in motion: which arteries feed the nidus, where the blood drains, whether there are aneurysms. Treatment is not planned without it.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="callout">
      <p>To assess surgical risk, neurosurgeons use the <strong>Spetzler–Martin scale</strong>. It considers three features: nidus size, proximity to functionally important brain areas (speech, movement, vision) and drainage into deep veins. The higher the score, the more complex and risky the removal.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="lechenie">
    <h2>Treatment</h2>
    <p>The goal of treatment is to exclude the AVM completely from the circulation. A partially excluded malformation still carries a risk of bleeding. There are three methods, often combined.</p>
    <h3>Microsurgical removal</h3>
    <p>Through a craniotomy under the microscope the feeding arteries are closed one by one, the nidus is dissected and removed entirely, with the draining vein last. The result is immediate. The method suits small AVMs away from functionally important areas.</p>
    <h3>Endovascular embolisation</h3>
    <p>Through a puncture of the artery in the groin a thin catheter is passed to the malformation and the nidus vessels are filled with a special glue. Sometimes this excludes the AVM completely, but more often embolisation is done before open surgery — to reduce flow and make removal safer.</p>
    <h3>Radiosurgery</h3>
    <p>The nidus is irradiated precisely with a single high dose. The vessels gradually close — this usually takes several years, and the risk of bleeding remains all that time. The method is chosen for small AVMs in deep or functionally important areas where surgery is too risky.</p>
    <h3>Observation</h3>
    <p>For some unruptured AVMs, especially large and deep ones, observation is safer than treatment. This decision is made together with the patient after weighing the risks.</p>
    <p>If an AVM has already bled, the risk of another bleed is higher and treatment becomes more pressing. I choose the final strategy based on angiography, and complex cases are discussed together with endovascular and radiosurgery specialists.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="posle">
    <h2>After treatment</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Follow-up angiography — to make sure the malformation is fully excluded. Timing depends on the method.</li>
      <li>If there were seizures, antiepileptic drugs continue; the decision to stop them is made by a neurologist.</li>
      <li>With neurological deficits — rehabilitation: exercise therapy and speech therapy under specialist supervision.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="srochno" slot="urgent">
    <h2>When to seek urgent help</h2>
    <div class="callout alert">
      <p>A sudden very severe headache, vomiting, weakness or numbness of half of the body, speech problems, seizures, loss of consciousness — <strong>call emergency services immediately (112; 103 in Russia).</strong></p>
    </div>
  </section>
